/**
@page cosmology_contracts Configuration, imports and output contracts

@section contracts_config Configuration

@ref cosmology::Config is parsed from `name=value` assignments. `#` and `//`
start comments; surrounding whitespace and an optional pair of double quotes
are removed. Unknown names, duplicate names, numeric trailing characters and
unsupported physics are errors. Parameters omitted from a file retain these
source defaults:

| Input key | Member | Default | Meaning/constraints |
|---|---|---|---|
| np | nGrid | 32 | Cells per dimension; even, at least 4; production also generates np^3 particles |
| nt | nSteps | 100 | Positive number of logarithmic scale-factor steps |
| box_size | boxSize | 200 | Positive comoving box length, Mpc/h |
| seed | seed | 1 | Unsigned 64-bit RNG seed |
| z_in / z_fi | zInitial / zFinal | 49 / 9 | Finite, z_in > z_fi >= 0 |
| hubble | hubble | 0.7 | Positive h, H0/(100 km/s/Mpc) |
| Omega_m | omegaMatter | 0.3 | Total matter, 0 < Omega_m <= 1 |
| Omega_bar | omegaBaryon | 0.05 | Transfer weighting, 0 <= Omega_bar <= Omega_m |
| Sigma_8 | sigma8 | 0.8 | Positive z=0 finite-cutoff RMS normalization |
| n_s | spectralIndex | 0.96 | Primordial tilt, 0 < n_s < 2 |
| TFFlag | transferFunction | 4 | 4 = BBKS; 0 = weighted transfer table |
| transfer_file | transferFile | empty | Required for flag 0; relative to parameter-file directory |
| ic_mode | icMode | gaussian | gaussian, sine, uniform or external |
| ic_file | icFile | empty | Complete canonical external phase-space file; relative to parameter file |
| particle_count | importedParticleCount | 0 | Required positive uint64 count for external IC; independent of np |
| snapshot_format | snapshotFormat | csv | csv or binary (IPPLPS01); no HDF5 in this stage |
| output_redshifts | outputRedshifts | empty | Comma-separated, strictly decreasing epochs in [z_fi,z_in) |
| amplitude | amplitude | 0.001 | Initial sine density amplitude, 0 <= A < 1; unused for other modes |
| mode_x/y/z | mode | (1,0,0) | Nonzero sine mode; every component strictly below Nyquist |
| output | output | cosmology-output | Output directory relative to run working directory |
| diagnostics_every | diagnosticsEvery | 10 | Positive diagnostic cadence |
| write_particles | writeParticles | true | Accepts true/false/1/0 |

Optional `Omega_nu`, `Omega_r` and `f_NL` must be exactly zero; `w_de` must be
exactly -1. They are accepted compatibility declarations, not enabled physics.
Grid bounds additionally protect uint64 particle counts and int Fourier-square
indices. @ref cosmology::Config::particleCount returns the production count;
imported evolution has a separate expected count when N_P differs from N_M.

@section contracts_production Production files

`Cosmology input.par` creates a fresh or empty output directory. It rejects
nonempty output to avoid accidental overwrite. Diagnostics are rank-zero CSV
rows with `step,a,D,f,particles,mass_error,delta_rms,mode_re,mode_im,
mode_amplitude,expected_amplitude,displacement_rms,momentum_rms,force_rms`.
The particle mode measurement is @f$N_p^{-1}\sum_p e^{-i\boldsymbol k\cdot x_p}@f$;
the cosine density amplitude is twice its magnitude for a nonzero mode. A
configured zero mode instead measures the DC particle-count sum; the diagnostic
does not subtract that DC component. The expected amplitude
uses linear growth and is not an analytical nonlinear prediction.

Snapshots are `particles_initial_rankR.csv` and `particles_final_rankR.csv`,
with `id,x,y,z,px,py,pz`. Ownership changes during migration: compare by global
ID, not by shard or row order. Positions are comoving Mpc/h, momenta canonical
as in @ref cosmology_model. `write_particles=false` suppresses these CSVs.
`metadata.txt` records conventions, configuration, rank/execution information,
maximum inverse imaginary residual and elapsed wall time. CSVs are diagnostic
artifacts, not scalable restart files. External input and binary epoch snapshots
are specified in @ref cosmology_quijote.

@section contracts_force Frozen-force adapter

`CompareCosmologyForce N L Omega_m input.csv output_dir` imports exactly N^3
unit-mass particles. The exact header is `id,x,y,z,mass`; IDs must be unique and
cover [0,N^3), values finite and masses exactly 1. Positions are wrapped into
[0,L), including multiple-box overshoots. Root validates input before MPI
scatter. Input rejection is broadcast so all ranks take the same failure path.
The adapter calls @ref cosmology::Simulation::compareFrozenForce and never
advances particles. It writes per-rank forces and deposited density/mesh forces
plus convention metadata. The comparison is against the full implemented
native operator, not merely force magnitudes.

@section contracts_evolution Imported evolution adapter

`CompareCosmologyEvolution NP NM L Omega_m ai af steps checkpoints input.csv output_dir`
uses NP^3 unit-weight particles on an NM^3 force mesh. The exact header is
`id,x,y,z,px,py,pz,mass`; IDs cover [0,NP^3), phase space is finite and mass=1.
The schedule requires 0 < ai < af <= 1, positive checkpoints and steps divisible
by checkpoints. Input size is bounded by MPI_Scatterv int counts. Root arrays
are diagnostic, local-memory-oriented adapters, not a distributed IC reader.

A checkpoint is synchronized after a complete second kick; checkpoint zero is
the actual imported state. Files are `particles_checkpointCCCC_rankR.csv` and
`checkpoints.csv`, with metadata. IPPL's checkpoint columns are
`checkpoint,step,a,mass_error,max_inverse_imaginary`. FastPM additionally records
particle count, mean momenta and E(a); consumers must not assume identical
schemas. The native reference stores momentum/acceleration in float32 and
positions/FFT data in float64. A half-cell coordinate rebase aligns its
node-centered PM convention with IPPL's cell-centered convention; native
forces and stepping themselves remain unchanged.

@section contracts_gadget GADGET conversion

The three-code comparison shares one ordered CSV, then converts it to
GADGET-2 format 1. Position blocks contain float32 kpc/h. GADGET input velocities
are @f$u=v_{\rm pec}/\sqrt a=100p/a^{3/2}@f$ in km/s, because its reader converts
them to internal canonical variables. Type 1 contains collisionless dark matter;
IDs are uint32, masses use 10^10 Msun/h, and the header is a 256-byte Fortran
record. Round-trip residuals and source/output hashes are retained. GADGET's
legacy short filename buffers require short relative paths; the local runner
starts it inside the campaign directory. Its final-format reader verifies
record markers, counts, IDs, finite values, box, background and final epoch.

@section contracts_errors Failure and provenance

Host validation failures raise exceptions; applications abort their MPI
communicator when peers could otherwise remain inside a collective. Scripts
return nonzero and retain logs/report states for partial runs. Existing results
are not repaired by changing acceptance tolerances. An analysis-only run checks
recorded hashes; source edits can invalidate a campaign's frozen source paths.
Keep the original source snapshot for historical audits. Data compression,
source provenance, campaign completion and physical qualification are distinct
contracts; see @ref cosmology_validation.
*/
